{"name":"Space Station","hex":"#6c6d7a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"space station","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A crewed artificial satellite designed for long-term habitation, research, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/space_station"}],"entries":[{"title":"Space station","blocks":[{"text":"A space station is a spacecraft which remains in orbit and hosts humans for extended periods of time. It is therefore an artificial satellite featuring habitation facilities. The purpose of maintaining a space station varies depending on the program. Most often space stations have been research stations, but they have also served military or commercial uses, such as hosting space tourists."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Space_station_size_comparison_as_of_November_2024.svg/330px-Space_station_size_comparison_as_of_November_2024.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_station","wikidata":"Q25956"},{"title":"Space Station (pinball)","blocks":[{"text":"Space Station is a 1987 Williams pinball machine designed by Barry Oursler."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a4/Space_Station_pinball_flyer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Station_(pinball)","wikidata":"Q136105440"},{"title":"Space","blocks":[{"text":"Space is a three-dimensional continuum containing positions and directions. In classical physics, physical space is often conceived in three linear dimensions. Modern physicists usually consider it, with time, to be part of a boundless four-dimensional continuum known as spacetime. The concept of space is considered to be of fundamental importance to an understanding of the physical universe. However, disagreement continues between philosophers over whether it is itself an entity, a relationship between entities, or part of a conceptual framework."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/3D_coordinate_system.svg/330px-3D_coordinate_system.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space","wikidata":"Q107"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Noordung_space_station.jpg/500px-Noordung_space_station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Noordung_space_station.jpg/1280px-Noordung_space_station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Noordung_space_station.jpg/500px-Noordung_space_station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"First description of a rotating space station, in Hermann Noordung's The Problem of Space Travel (1929).(Legend: Achs-Körper: axle body. Aufzugschacht: elevator shaft. Treppenschacht: stairwell. Verdampfungsrohr: boiler pipe).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANoordung_space_station.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Gemini8Docking.gif/330px-Gemini8Docking.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Gemini8Docking.gif/330px-Gemini8Docking.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gemini 8 docking with Agena vehicle","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGemini8Docking.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Skylab_3_flyaround.jpg/500px-Skylab_3_flyaround.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Skylab_3_flyaround.jpg/1280px-Skylab_3_flyarou